          "secNumber": "1.1",
          "heading": "Foundational Principles & Theory: Reaction Kinetics and Rate Laws",
          "content": "**Scope:** Rate laws, reaction order, temperature, mechanisms.\n\n## Competencies\n\nBy the end of this unit, a reader should translate an operating observation into a defensible model, solve a numerical case using consistent units, identify the most influential assumption, and explain what evidence would invalidate the prediction. All calculations are teaching examples unless separately identified as standards-compliant design.\n\n## Unit learning sequence\n\n- Lesson 1: Rate definitions and stoichiometric consistency\n- Lesson 2: Reaction order and rate-law interpretation\n- Lesson 3: Temperature effects and Arrhenius parameters\n- Lesson 4: Mechanisms and pseudo-steady intermediates\n\n## Applied design studio\n\nKinetic package for a pilot reaction. Design a temperature-and-concentration test matrix, estimate apparent reaction order, and supply an Arrhenius sensitivity analysis. Deliver signed species balances, raw-assay tables, and an uncertainty budget; explicitly distinguish kinetic effects from transport limitations.\n\n## Measurement and reporting quality\n\nA credible laboratory report separates raw data, corrected data, numerical predictions and observations. Preserve the original concentration basis and initial conditions. Label all time axes and distinguish a measured temperature from an assumed constant. Provide an uncertainty interval or at least a sensitivity range for the parameter controlling the decision. For safe desk exercises synthetic data are acceptable if labeled as such. Avoid suggesting unsupervised hazardous experiments or relying on classroom calculations for relief sizing.\n\n### Lesson 1: Rate definitions and stoichiometric consistency\n\nRead [full lesson](../lessons/unit-01-lesson-01.html). Core equation: `-r_A = k C_A^n`.\n\n### Lesson 2: Reaction order and rate-law interpretation\n\nRead [full lesson](../lessons/unit-01-lesson-02.html). Core equation: `r = k C_A^m C_B^n`.\n\n### Lesson 3: Temperature effects and Arrhenius parameters\n\nRead [full lesson](../lessons/unit-01-lesson-03.html). Core equation: `ln(k_2/k_1) = -(E_a/R)(1/T_2-1/T_1)`.\n\n### Lesson 4: Mechanisms and pseudo-steady intermediates\n\nRead [full lesson](../lessons/unit-01-lesson-04.html). Core equation: `dC_I/dt \u2248 0; r = k_1 C_A k_2/(k_{-1}+k_2)`."
