          "content": "# Unit 1 project \u2014 Kinetic package for a pilot reaction\n\n## Decision brief\n\nDesign a temperature-and-concentration test matrix, estimate apparent reaction order, and supply an Arrhenius sensitivity analysis. Deliver signed species balances, raw-assay tables, and an uncertainty budget; explicitly distinguish kinetic effects from transport limitations.\n\n## Inputs and provenance\n\nUse clearly labeled synthetic data or independently verified measurements. For kinetic data, provide temperature and concentration units and a method for estimating variability. For operating studies, identify volumetric flow reference states and a clearly bounded control volume. Each property value needs its source, temperature and composition. Do not use reaction safety screening as a substitute for a required hazard study.\n\n## Deliverables\n\n1. A process flow schematic with each boundary, measured variable and manipulated control.\n2. A transparent mathematical model: species balances, an energy balance where relevant, parameters and numerical solver tolerances.\n3. At least two physically feasible alternatives evaluated on identical input and output bases.\n4. Four numerical checks: conservation, dimensional consistency, a limiting case, and sensitivity to the most uncertain parameter.\n5. A testable prediction about operation under a realistic perturbation, with a criterion that would reject the model.\n6. A limitations register identifying missing kinetics, transport, physical-property and safeguards evidence.\n\n## Review questions\n\nExplain why the selected architecture is robust to its dominant uncertainty. Identify an apparently attractive but misleading performance metric and calculate the consequence of improving it at the expense of another. Explain how a tracer experiment, thermal test, feed assay or controlled startup could resolve a key uncertainty. Finally, state why the desk-study result must not authorize equipment operation or process-safety decisions.\n\n## Suggested evaluation rubric\n\nMass/energy accounting 25%; model assumptions and evidence 20%; correct calculations 20%; exploration of alternatives 15%; uncertainty and limitations 15%; readable reports and reproducibility 5%."
